INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A concrete tower, nine stories tall, hollow as a throat. A rusted catwalk spirals up the inside wall. Chutes hang like dead tongues. Wind through broken louvres makes a sound like breathing.

CALEB WRIGHT, thirties, a tow-truck driver, leads with a flashlight. AMARA OKORO, thirties, his girlfriend, follows with a phone. DEACON PRICE, sixties, a retired farmhand, carries a lantern. LIV HAGEN, twenties, a student, clutches a camera. PEPPER NAKAMURA, forties, a county sheriff's deputy, brings up the rear with a hand on her holster.

CALEB
Stay on the catwalk.

AMARA
It's moving.

DEACON
That's just the wind, son.

LIV
Wind doesn't whisper.

PEPPER
Everybody quiet.

They stop. Something below clatters. Grain trickles from a chute and hisses across the floor though the silo has been empty for twenty years.

AMARA
Caleb.

CALEB
I see it.

DEACON
Back up, all of you.

LIV
Did somebody say my name?

PEPPER
Nobody said your name.

LIV
Somebody did.

Deacon holds up the lantern. The flame leans toward the dark at the bottom of the tower. Caleb sweeps his beam across the floor. Piles of grain stir like sleepers.

AMARA
It's pulling the grain.

CALEB
Pulling it where?

DEACON
Toward the hatch.

PEPPER
What hatch?

DEACON
The one under the scale house.

They all look. A black square gapes in the concrete, ringed with old stains. A cold breath rises.

LIV
Deputy, shouldn't you call somebody?

PEPPER
No signal.

AMARA
Mine neither.

CALEB
Then we leave. Now.

He turns and starts up the catwalk. The others follow. Behind them, the grain heaves up in a wave and breaks against the wall.

DEACON
Run.

They scramble up the spiral, boots ringing on the iron, the catwalk shaking. Liv drops her camera. Amara grabs her sleeve. Pepper draws and fires once into the dark, the shot huge, and nothing answers.

CALEB
Keep climbing!

The door at the top is chained. He hits it with his shoulder. It does not give. Pepper puts her pistol against the lock and fires. The chain falls. The door bursts open on a starry night.

They pour out onto the roof. Deacon slams the door and braces his back against it.

DEACON
Is everyone here?

LIV
Five.

AMARA
Four. Where's Pepper?

They turn. The door buckles from below, once, twice. Something presses a hand against the inside of the steel. The metal bulges in the shape of fingers.

PEPPER (O.S.)
(muffled, calm)
Don't open it.

AMARA
Pepper!

PEPPER (O.S.)
Don't open it. I'm not the one knocking.
